    "content": "A Freeze Warning is issued by the National Weather Service (NWS) when surface temperatures are expected to drop to or below 32∘F (0∘C) during the growing season. These warnings indicate a high likelihood of widespread freezing temperatures within the next 24 to 36 hours, capable of damaging or killing sensitive vegetation and agricultural crops. Key Takeaways: Freeze Warning: Temperatures ≤32∘F expected. Hard Freeze Warning: Temperatures ≤28∘F expected, which often kills most types of vegetation. Frost Advisory: Issued when temperatures are expected to be 33∘F to 36∘F on clear, calm nights, causing patchy frost. Timing: These are issued during the local growing season. A Freeze Watch is issued 12 to 48 hours before a potential freeze, signaling that conditions are favorable for freezing, but it is not yet guaranteed.",
